🔄 Combine vs Async/Await in SwiftUI (Day 6)

SwiftUI supports two async paradigms:

Combine (Reactive streams)
Async/Await (Structured concurrency)

A common interview question is:

“Which one should you use and when?”

🧠 Core Difference
Combine
Event stream based (Publisher → Subscriber)
Async/Await
Sequential async code (like sync code)
1️⃣ Basic Comparison
	Combine	Async/Await
Style	Reactive	Sequential
Complexity	Higher	Lower
Learning curve	Steep	Easy
Cancellation	Manual	Built-in
Readability	Lower	High
2️⃣ Combine Example
publisher
    .sink { value in
        print(value)
    }
    .store(in: &cancellables)

This is a continuous stream.

3️⃣ Async/Await Example
func load() async {
    let data = await fetch()
    print(data)
}

This is a single async operation.

4️⃣ When to Use Async/Await ✅

Use async/await for:

API calls
One-time data fetch
User-triggered actions
Simple workflows

Example:

.task {
    await viewModel.load()
}
5️⃣ When to Use Combine ✅

Use Combine for:

Continuous data streams
Observing changes
Real-time updates
Complex event pipelines

Example:

$text
    .debounce(for: .milliseconds(300), scheduler: RunLoop.main)
    .sink { value in
        search(value)
    }
6️⃣ SwiftUI Internally Uses Combine

Even if you don’t use it directly:

@Published

Uses Combine under the hood.

7️⃣ Bridging Between Them
Combine → Async
let value = try await publisher.values.first
Async → Combine
Future { promise in
    Task {
        let result = await fetch()
        promise(.success(result))
    }
}
8️⃣ Cancellation Comparison
Combine
cancellable.cancel()
Async/Await
Task {
    await load()
}.cancel()

SwiftUI also auto-cancels .task.

9️⃣ Real-World Pattern (Best Practice)

Modern SwiftUI apps:

View → async/await
ViewModel → async/await
State updates → Combine (@Published)

👉 Hybrid approach

🔥 Common Mistakes
Overusing Combine for simple tasks
Mixing both unnecessarily
Ignoring cancellation
Writing complex Combine chains unnecessarily
🧠 Interview Power Answer

If asked:

“Should we still use Combine in SwiftUI?”

Answer:

“Yes, but primarily for reactive streams. For most async operations, I prefer async/await because it’s simpler and integrates better with SwiftUI.”

⭐ Decision Rule
Single async task → async/await  
Continuous stream → Combine
💡 Senior-Level Insight

Async/Await replaced many Combine use cases, but Combine is still essential for event streams and reactive pipelines.

